{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794515053,"nonce":"4moHoIiPvzcR4UDq+SheEdmzb4A=","title":"Where compute shaders could help the GPU deferral plan (122c606)","message":"Assessed from the plan text, not measured.\n\nFits, weakly: P2b and the hierarchy walk. One dispatch over the node table (at most 1024 rows) could evaluate the descriptors from the frame clock and resolve each row to its absolute composed transform and opacity once per frame, writing a flat table. The vertex stage then does one fetch per primitive, chains deeper than 4 stop falling back to the CPU, and easing and spring maths do not repeat per vertex. Against it: the data is tiny, the A14 is fill-bound not vertex-bound (36c7812), compute exists only where storage buffers do (tier 0, so the tier 1 and 2 CPU patcher stays), and on tile-based GPUs a compute pass before the render pass is a pass boundary every frame. Do not start here.\n\nAdjacent, not in the plan:\n- Glyph atlas on the GPU (vector or SDF glyphs): the plan notes glyph bitmaps are resampled when scaled up, so moving text goes soft. Generating the atlas by compute would keep scaled text sharp. Quality, not CPU. First candidate if compute is wanted.\n- Compute culling with indirect draws: a long list's window would need no CPU repaint, but every row's primitives must be resident (about 56 MB for 100k rows at 560 bytes each), and virtual_list already avoids it by not building the rows. Low value while virtual lists exist.\n\nCheck before any of it: wgpu compute support per tier and target (WebGL2 has none), and that a pre-pass dispatch does not split the render pass on the PowerVR and Apple targets.","files":null}]}